  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1147 PM CST Wed Nov 11 2026\n\nLake Effect Showers Across Northern Wisconsin...\n\nContinued cold air advection from the north will bring lake \neffect snow showers into the region Thursday through Friday night.\nHow far inland these showers get throughout the day Thursday and \nFriday will vary, but the majority of any accumulations will be \nacross north- central Vilas County, with only trace to dusting \namounts making their way further inland. Accumulations will be \nprimarily limited during the day by above freezing surface \ntemperatures and winds, so although a few inches of snowfall are \nin the forecast, actual surface accumulation probabilities are \nlower, with around 30% chance of exceeding 2 inches of snowfall \nacross Vilas County. If snow accumulation proves more robust (10% \nworst case), accumulations could get up to 3 to 4 inches over the \ncourse of the 48 hour period.\n\nLake Effect for Lake Michigan...\n\nLake effect showers will also be underway over Lake michigan as\nthe northerly winds increase. The main areas to watch will be Door\nand Kewaunee counties, which may see a rapid increase in\nprecipitation if these showers get enough of an easterly \ncomponent to them to bring the banded snowfall onshore. These \nareas are also unlikely to see snow mix in until the overnight \nperiod late tonight, with pure snow likely after midnight. That \nsaid, if this band does move inland overnight, impactful snowfall \ncould be in the works for portions of Door and northern Kewaunee \ncounties. Lake induced CAPE around 200-300 J/kg, Delta-T around 15\ndegrees, and good mixing through the lowest layer near the lake \nsurface would all support concern for a push of higher snow totals\nover a relatively localized area, as lake effect snow bands are \ntypically quite narrow. In the current 10% worst case, this could \nbring a faster 4+ inches in 12 hours, which would merit the first \nWinter Weather Advisory of the season. Confidence of all this \nlining up is increasing over the last couple forecast cycles but \nstill only sits at around 30% on the probabilistic side, as \np-types over Door do switch back to rain during the latter part of\nthe day Friday. Therefore, will hold off on any headlines at this\njuncture, but where the radar returns over Lake Michigan and the \nBay of Green Bay orient themselves over the next 24 hours will be \nimportant to monitor.\n\nFurther Inland...\n\nThe rest of the region may see some sprinkles or even flurries at\ntimes, with the flurries more likely during the overnight period\nThursday night into Friday morning as the air column gets colder.\nFirst possible snowflakes region-wide aside, impacts for the rest\nof the region will be fairly minimal.\n\nRest of the Forecast...\n\nQuiet conditions are expected to return to the region over the\nweekend. High temperatures that will be near normal Thursday and\nFriday will see a slow rise going into next week, getting back\ntowards the 50s across the region.",
